`geom_smooth(method = "lm")` draws the same fitted line as `lm()`, with a shaded confidence band around it.

::: {when-level="moderate"}

## Multiple Regression

Adding more predictors lets the model account for other sources of variation at the same time.


::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
library(gtsummary)

model <- lm(blood_pressure ~ age + treatment, data = data)
summary(model)
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-stdout}

```

Call:
lm(formula = blood_pressure ~ age + treatment, data = data)

Residuals:
    Min      1Q  Median      3Q     Max 
-41.450  -9.232   1.240   9.875  36.964 

Coefficients:
             Estimate Std. Error t value Pr(>|t|)    
(Intercept) 118.86733    7.64781  15.543   <2e-16 ***
age           0.02301    0.14337   0.161    0.873    
treatmentB   -0.22012    3.05880  -0.072    0.943    
---
Signif. codes:  0 '***' 0.001 '**' 0.01 '*' 0.05 '.' 0.1 ' ' 1

Residual standard error: 15.27 on 97 degrees of freedom
Multiple R-squared:  0.0003302,	Adjusted R-squared:  -0.02028 
F-statistic: 0.01602 on 2 and 97 DF,  p-value: 0.9841
```


:::

```{.r .cell-code}
# Using gtsummary for a publication-ready table
tbl_regression(model)
```

